[Id "french"]
[Name "French Defense"]
[Side "black"]
[ECO "C00-C19"]
[Description "Solid defence: 1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5, then hit the centre with …c5."]
[Tags "solid, positional"]

1. e4 e6 {…e6 prepares …d5, with a pawn ready to recapture on d5.} 2. d4
  (2. Nf3 {@variation 2.Nf3} 2... d5 {…d5 claims the centre, just as against 2.d4.} 3. exd5 exd5 {…exd5: when White plays d4, this is the Exchange Variation.}
{@line id=french-2nf3 level=1 tier=side name="2.Nf3" summary="Against 2.Nf3, still play …d5; after exd5 exd5 it's an Exchange French."}
)
2... d5 3. e5 {@variation Advance}
  (3. exd5 {@variation Exchange} 3... exd5 {…exd5 gives a symmetrical structure, and Black's light-squared bishop is free.} 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bd3 Bd6 {…Bd6 mirrors White's development; the position is level.} 6. O-O O-O
{@line id=french-exchange-main level=1 tier=main name="Exchange Variation" summary="Symmetrical and calm: develop naturally and castle."}
)
  (3. Nc3 {@variation Winawer} 3... Bb4 {…Bb4 pins the c3 knight, adding pressure on e4.} 4. e5 c5 {…c5 hits the d4 pawn, the classic French counter.} 5. a3 Bxc3+ {…Bxc3+ gives up the bishop to double White's c-pawns.} 6. bxc3 Ne7 {…Ne7 develops and keeps the f-pawn free; the knight can go to f5 or c6.}
{@line id=french-winawer-main level=1 tier=main name="Winawer Variation" summary="Pin with …Bb4, hit d4 with …c5, then trade on c3 to damage White's pawns."}
7. Qg4 {Qg4 attacks g7, which the dark-squared bishop no longer defends. [%cal Rg4g7]}
    (7. Nf3 Nbc6 {…Nbc6 adds pressure on d4 against White's quiet set-up.} (7... b6 {@alt})
{@line id=french-winawer-nf3 level=2 tier=side name="7.Nf3" summary="Against the quiet 7.Nf3, keep hitting d4 with …Nbc6; …b6 and …Ba6 to trade bishops is also good."}
)
  7... Qc7 {…Qc7 counterattacks e5 and c3 instead of defending g7.} 8. Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qxh7 cxd4 {…cxd4 opens the c-file; Black gives kingside pawns for a strong centre attack.} 10. Ne2 Nbc6 11. f4 Bd7 {…Bd7 prepares …O-O-O, so the black king hides on the queenside.}
{@line id=french-winawer-qg4 level=2 tier=main name="7.Qg4 (Poisoned Pawn)" summary="Let White take g7 and h7: hit c3 and the centre, then castle long."}
)
  (3. Nd2 {@variation Tarrasch} 3... c5 {…c5 hits the centre at once; Black accepts an isolated d-pawn for active pieces.} 4. exd5 exd5 5. Ngf3 Nc6 {…Nc6 develops and adds pressure on d4.} 6. Bb5 Bd6 {…Bd6 develops towards White's kingside.}
{@line id=french-tarrasch-open level=1 tier=side name="Open (3…c5)" summary="Meet 3.Nd2 with …c5: active pieces in return for an isolated d-pawn."}
)
3... c5 {…c5 strikes at d4, the base of White's pawn chain.} 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 {…Qb6 piles up on d4 and eyes the b2 pawn.} 6. a3
  (6. Bd3 {Bd3 develops and offers the d4 pawn: the Milner-Barry Gambit.} 6... cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 {…Bd7 first: taking on d4 now loses the queen to Bb5+. [%cal Rd3b5]} (7... Nxd4 {@mistake Nxd4 Qxd4 Bb5+ and White wins your queen. [%cal Rd3b5]})
{@line id=french-advance-bd3 level=1 tier=main name="Milner-Barry (6.Bd3)" summary="Against 6.Bd3, trade on d4 and play …Bd7 before taking anything: Bb5+ is the trap."}
8. O-O
    (8. Bc2 Nb4 {…Nb4 hits the c2 bishop and eyes d3. [%cal Gb4c2]}
{@line id=french-advance-mb-bc2 level=2 tier=side name="Milner-Barry: 8.Bc2" summary="If White keeps the bishop with Bc2, jump into b4 with the knight."}
)
  8... Nxd4 {…Nxd4 takes the offered pawn, now that Bb5+ is covered.} 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 a6 {…a6 stops Nb5, which would hit the queen and c7. [%cal Rc3b5]} (10... Qxe5 {@mistake Re1 and Nb5 chase your queen while White develops.})
{@line id=french-advance-milner-barry level=2 tier=main name="Milner-Barry Gambit" summary="With …Bd7 in, the d4 pawn is safe to take; then …a6 stops Nb5."}
)
  (6. Be2 cxd4 {…cxd4 opens the c-file and leaves d4 without its c3 support.} 7. cxd4 Nh6 {…Nh6 heads for f5, where the knight attacks d4. [%cal Gh6f5]} (7... Nge7 {@alt}) 8. O-O Nf5 {…Nf5 adds a third attacker on d4, next to the c6 knight and the b6 queen.}
{@line id=french-advance-be2 level=2 tier=main name="6.Be2" summary="Trade on d4, then bring the g8 knight via h6 to f5 to pile up on the d4 pawn."}
)
6... c4 {…c4 clamps the queenside, taking b3 and d3 away from White.} (6... Bd7 {@alt})
{@line id=french-advance-main level=1 tier=main name="Advance Variation" summary="Attack the base of the chain: …c5, …Nc6 and …Qb6 all hit d4."}
7. Be2
  (7. Nbd2 Na5 {…Na5 heads for b3 as usual. [%cal Ga5b3]} 8. Be2 Bd7 {…Bd7: the same position as the 7.Be2 order.}
{@line id=french-advance-a3-nbd2 level=2 tier=side name="6.a3 c4 7.Nbd2" summary="7.Nbd2 and 7.Be2 lead to the same position: …Na5 and …Bd7 either way."}
)
7... Na5 {…Na5 heads for b3, a strong square White can no longer guard with a pawn. [%cal Ga5b3]} 8. Nbd2 Bd7 {…Bd7 develops and supports …Bb5 or …Nb3 later.}
{@line id=french-advance-a3 level=2 tier=main name="6.a3 c4 7.Be2" summary="After …c4, the queenside is Black's: aim the knight at b3 and keep White's b4 in check."} *
